#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f98628a-6f9a-5780-bcb7-c4159ebffbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 6 u^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556175} {:answer/id #uuid "4db4a637-66c3-5be5-b547-b65d4a25ba17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 6 u^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556181} {:answer/id #uuid "859f120a-9f52-53a5-b21e-43a5ca135f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} + 6 u^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556188} {:answer/id #uuid "a204bddf-b5a3-54b0-b771-a846e11c4fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 u^{3} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{2} v \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} + 3 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556193} {:answer/id #uuid "e37f0c30-7b20-56ac-862d-6815c74c117d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 u^{2} + u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} + 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556175} :answer-field/id #uuid "632c495b-e0b5-5c31-9a2a-2149c703d3a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e15ae53-ba38-57f6-8022-32b428df3df5" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle 3 x,\\,y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,2 u^{2}\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0,\\ 0<v<2\\pi\\}$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(2 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,\\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,4 u\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(2 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&4 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&4 u\\\\u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&4 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 4 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 4 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle 3 x,\\,y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,y=u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,z=2 u^{2}} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3 \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,\\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,3 \\left(2 u^{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,6 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,6 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle - 4 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 4 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 4 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 4 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 u^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(12 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 12 u^{3}\\right)+\\left(- 4 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 u^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 6 u^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 6 u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fd7c6ed-0049-55d7-9bd9-16c6d845e842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556182} {:answer/id #uuid "a030b89c-aa1b-5a79-868b-a1c76698d820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} + 4 u^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556192} {:answer/id #uuid "a985faa1-671a-54ed-a6f6-6ac81360abc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 u^{2} + 3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} + 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556196} {:answer/id #uuid "efac243a-6cd8-59e9-8f94-8255fb41bff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556204} {:answer/id #uuid "f188a79c-9a08-5271-ae2e-8ab1ce201061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 16 u^{3} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} - 24 u^{2} v \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} + 3 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556182} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1464911-c671-53b3-b177-a7a25bd50aef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fbab4c98-3680-578c-b61c-cce609750ccc" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle 2 x,\\,3 y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,4 u^{2}\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0,\\ 0<v<2\\pi\\}$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(4 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,\\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,8 u\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(4 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&8 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&8 u\\\\u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&8 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 8 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle 2 x,\\,3 y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,y=u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,z=4 u^{2}} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2 \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,3 \\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,3 \\left(4 u^{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,12 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,12 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle - 8 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 u^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 16 u^{3}\\right)+\\left(- 24 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 u^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =- 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D - 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc03413-e383-5579-b76c-092cfdf0ba2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557459} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf1d1bd-cd3b-5362-a201-96e2f8d65215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557477} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dd696a-a439-5722-8165-12e98bab773e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557484} {:answer/id #uuid "cf207c00-d6db-5ca5-ae88-332c78997858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557486} {:answer/id #uuid "f0551450-197d-58b1-8016-b5ae5d19fb42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557484} :answer-field/id #uuid "b06b705a-a49e-57b4-8e75-93b51d1cd651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "122c57cf-cfe9-5e24-b30b-49b9810a796b" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 4x, y = 5x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\,xy=7,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & xy=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc524083463c8c370ff1f79d68093f0d3f2c2fe1ceb5cd47824782e35a0bac9.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (7-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7679f101-3932-576a-bb5f-e5c4e0d8f328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557469} {:answer/id #uuid "b146b24c-8adf-51d6-9b4a-ace2765fa126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557481} {:answer/id #uuid "b8309c86-ea8f-5023-84d6-cc1e1d1ff7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557483} {:answer/id #uuid "d5935ed1-7de1-54f7-8776-fa2897e513ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557488} {:answer/id #uuid "fe94daf5-1f4e-5c5e-a0ad-b319ec84510e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557488} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b4bdfb4-f617-5835-a9b7-bf4aa30c0245" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e54c2fb-3e60-532d-b51f-b6a974c7d6cf" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = \\frac{5}{x}, y = 2x, y = 3x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=2,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cdd0431c256b72ded23c14fac4098b518a46603d46d5dfd238c54b3a46c66db.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73eb65d7-09df-500e-9d56-1a66efa416ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557468} {:answer/id #uuid "7c271fcc-050b-5bd7-bb0a-bd7e59bd8013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557472} {:answer/id #uuid "abbdfe07-8aed-512d-9ff0-01181e26a377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557478} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8c388f-4bcc-5ca9-9c82-9cdfdfa398e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557492} {:answer/id #uuid "fb51af7c-e436-58e4-828c-830ac59f2a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557472} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe81a033-5b30-59ce-b828-9ec0ad8c66db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "21d697ec-a429-5e49-b479-9da388eda962" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x=2{y}^{2},\\,x=3{y}^{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=2,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cdd0431c256b72ded23c14fac4098b518a46603d46d5dfd238c54b3a46c66db.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bde0c95-87d9-5021-b422-7d119cac0a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557446} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6c0a49-01f3-50ea-b745-5a7c5cfa9970" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557455} {:answer/id #uuid "2e012937-cb75-5afb-