x}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 5,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/56/560d4d074d9d07eb034d92b8f1dfabfc3e59d659c91d80c3fedf68367a575e41.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5-4)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f03a8f3-09d3-5671-890f-62e7bfebe2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557457} {:answer/id #uuid "6a93d690-2db3-54db-8e20-261a49e04fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557467} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c76cd2-18bc-54c4-bec6-41c9e208d51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557480} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff2435-f7c2-57f1-a4e1-b9ad0070da42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557490} {:answer/id #uuid "f72a6040-f6eb-5203-b778-5f0e5d0eb63b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557495} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f802c8-f27c-572f-a462-5834e5ade5cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df897641-0828-516d-b42d-f7e72c630377" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\frac{3}{x}, y = \\frac{7}{x}, y = 3x, y = 5x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\,xy=7,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & xy=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/cf/cf598751d1bb1edda1bb65fd5b94d2ad5805289c48716f69a71394fd3684e920.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (7-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18aef37b-13fc-5985-b171-ee79226ba9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{273 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557803} {:answer/id #uuid "68ea381c-9350-5902-8a2d-5365327562c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{63 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557818} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfac82d-50a4-585f-89af-6ef3edfdc45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 84 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557819} {:answer/id #uuid "8db76247-e4b0-5546-a657-8f8e6e8388d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{105 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557826} {:answer/id #uuid "a135d7b3-50aa-5afe-84ad-61682c19e240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557818} :answer-field/id #uuid "59997270-8b69-57f0-869d-fe6b2aa3f380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "03ef4d10-8b30-5c10-920c-534b995f0855" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + 16 y^{2}=144$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(4,0)$ to the point $(-4,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\pi].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\left(4 x^{2} y - 2 y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(3 x y^{2} + 2 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\left(192 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(108 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\left(- 768 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(24 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(324 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(- 444 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 24\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\left(\\frac{111 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{63}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{63 t}{2} + \\frac{111 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right]_{0}^{\\pi}\\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(\\pi\\right)}{2} + \\frac{111 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(0\\right)}{2} + \\frac{111 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{63 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\frac{63 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f4ba88-6906-5807-8e56-38b9cac7040e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{93 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557808} {:answer/id #uuid "52882614-2c8f-5d4c-bf34-552065d5c6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557810} {:answer/id #uuid "6431ed13-3ebd-5c26-b688-c8a633f096b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557817} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e27914-31ba-5ff7-a5fa-85eb69175863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{99 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557847} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b63cf9-e33e-5263-b5aa-e288270277d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{99}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557848}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557808} :answer-field/id #uuid "45310c5c-a7e1-5c63-9f05-e64c2f30ae51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0faff4ca-cb6c-5f59-80aa-48ecec1fa2a8" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(108 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(117 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{93}{8} - \\frac{117 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{93 t}{8} - \\frac{117 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{32}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{93 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{8} - \\frac{117 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{93 \\left(0\\right)}{8} - \\frac{117 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(\\frac{93 \\pi}{16}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{93 \\pi}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e7f72d-414d-53aa-8ef8-cead044d1e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 84 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557804} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7e14b-101e-5f42-b58e-9a1bd14174ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(78 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557840} {:answer/id #uuid "e662a31f-cab2-56f1-9681-41eae72c7042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(81 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557844} {:answer/id #uuid "e925186a-0214-5007-b114-7ce6da66f8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557845} {:answer/id #uuid "eb76069a-19c5-5b49-baed-4f9249748312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 81 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557840} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0404c5-2336-5ed6-a42f-c0ecfbbeabd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73913116-384d-502d-a4e5-8830e55db474" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- x y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- x y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 27 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(81 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(78 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0826fc-9ba4-5530-9d52-a358f5c90ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557801} {:answer/id #uuid "8634f0b6-6781-50a6-9009-8b052ccd87c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 15 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557823} {:answer/id #uuid "9583d382-ea32-56a5-9d1a-745663042e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 63 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557828} {:answer/id #uuid "aa610211-4e68-569f-99ec-1ba9534cea16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557833} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb74e83-07bd-502b-b98b-06c2a15eae60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 39 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557823} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c22627-b132-5666-b74d-b94aa86cc293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8739eb28-3666-500d-b030-f6ce2af45845" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $16 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=144$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $4,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 4 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- 3 x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 144 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 144 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(432 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 576 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 144 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(18 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 30\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- 30 t + \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(- 30 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)-\\left(- 30 \\left(0\\right) + \\frac{9 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 15 \\pi\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 15 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3847c491-e0a0-55fb-b8ed-745703409a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{39 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557806} {:answer/id #uuid "5c279f43-81b1-5048-a51f-9dec9c1853d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557814} {:answer/id #uuid "aecc94f3-c0b8-59f4-a6e5-f71ef3d37ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{63 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557834} {:answer/id #uuid "b9552b1b-4bf6-5bfc-8c85-7313d734f76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557838} {:answer/id #uuid "d4747d89-0358-5ecd-8c62-f05dadd92f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{51 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557834} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ff7999-cf9a-5af9-84d4-39d73a6002f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9be25acc-1ec0-58da-aef8-6711e4d01192" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + 4 y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- 2 x y^{2} - 4 x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 36 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(48 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 24 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 108 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 60 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 24\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{15 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{2} - \\frac{63}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{63 t}{2} + \\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{2} + \\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{63 \\left(0\\right)}{2} + \\frac{15 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{63 \\pi}{4}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\frac{63 \\pi}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a63d515-0ab8-5468-81ea-d6a8e74ac4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{57}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557813} {:answer/id #uuid "5e778ab1-5d99-51ad-8b59-306deab0c03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{57 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557815} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba88d97-7753-513f-a98a-5762e70fbde1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557821} {:answer/id #uuid "92409415-01eb-5113-8034-f7c988147bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21 \\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557827} {:answer/id #uuid "987fcf17-c5ad-5319-b164-9e0a387a95d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{15 \\pi}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557821} :answer-field/id #uuid "5277df30-4b80-52ef-a167-017b9064a2dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b760d270-0b52-5110-b068-ec2545a8607e" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 2 x^{2} y + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- x y^{2} - x\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(51 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{27}{8} - \\frac{51 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{8}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{27 t}{8} - \\frac{51 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{32}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{27 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}{8} - \\frac{51 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{27 \\left(0\\right)}{8} - \\frac{51 \\sin{\\left(4 \\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{32}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(\\frac{27 \\pi}{16}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{27 \\pi}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0336cc-9fd9-5b2d-9a57-64a24aef91a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557805} {:answer/id #uuid "5a48c3f8-c214-51b9-b123-90b0d948c8e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557812} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9b686a-2146-5d69-bd8e-5d06c4ab2905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557822} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b5a01a-cd71-58fa-a674-5b6e3a5dec58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557835} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dfce80-11c9-593e-8b53-5e8284197d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{33 \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557835} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d8f57ae-5aa7-5339-8cec-7f584bbc1313" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8886af2-15c3-5d9c-8be1-602af41bdf96" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j,\\quad t\\in[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$r\\prime(t)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j.$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(- 4 x + y\\right)x\\prime(t)+\\left(- x + 3 y\\right)y\\prime(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(39 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{39 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ & =\\left[- 3 t + \\frac{39 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\\\ & =\\left(- 3 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\frac{39 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) + \\frac{39 \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{39}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{39}{2} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01570206-5cdd-5e6b-9104-73e9fcfb4d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{u v}\\left(4 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558766} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ad14c-de95-56d5-905f-adc4c1595467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(4 w\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558779} {:answer/id #uuid "7208a6f8-db4c-57ff-b758-167c5cbcf08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u + v}}\\left(4 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558787} {:answer/id #uuid "b540dafe-8822-5831-96de-99fe23d46ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(2 w\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558796} {:answer/id #uuid "c66fca7b-36c4-56cb-8c0c-ac1faa865c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(4 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558800} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bae0846-9e7d-581b-a860-26c008b5e751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b76035f-4083-5963-886f-519463991382" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x + y\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le x - y\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le z\\le \\left(x - y\\right) \\left(x + y\\right)\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 1\\le w\\le \\sqrt{u v}\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0\\\\1 & -1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}-(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ Since $z\\ge1$ and $w=\\sqrt z$, the branch is $w>0$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(4 w\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(4 w\\right)\\left|- w\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(4 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026d0fdb-c581-5805-a7ce-956120cb2488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(\\frac{- 12 u - v w}{16 v}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558767} {:answer/id #uuid "1d254e30-5cb5-5b46-93cb-81d3d3153a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(- \\frac{3 u}{4} - \\frac{w}{4}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558770} {:answer/id #uuid "7b55cdc3-8c0c-5ff2-b9be-cfd98c564cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(- \\frac{3 u}{4} - \\frac{w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558789} {:answer/id #uuid "834210eb-ce4e-561d-8caa-3539a509f6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(\\frac{v \\left(12 u + w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558790} {:answer/id #uuid "a09b199f-4fbe-567e-bbbf-f1eda74093e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(v^{2} \\left(- 12 u - w\\right)\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558789} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b93097b-db4a-5d04-a26f-2558e108a845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a980a84-b104-5f71-8ad4-d675189b5a6a" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x y\\le 2,\\quad -2\\le y\\le -1,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z,\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 x y^{2} + y z\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 2,\\quad -2\\le v\\le -1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 8\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0\\\\0 & 1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(y)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(x)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=4 y\\\\ &=4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4 v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ The sign of $v$ is fixed throughout $Δ$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 x y^{2} + y z\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(12 u + w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(12 u + w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{1}{4 v}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{8}\\left(- \\frac{3 u}{4} - \\frac{w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3df91c-acac-5c15-a4be-f4c02430118e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(\\frac{3 w}{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558771} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6a0298-2596-572e-b463-351496792e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(3 w\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558777} {:answer/id #uuid "55eff19e-64a0-58c9-a3ac-3a4127bd2f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(3 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558781} {:answer/id #uuid "6f82d57c-839e-59bf-be77-b81ccfd2d25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{u v}\\left(3 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558786} {:answer/id #uuid "b82588e5-cfb7-5f6d-81d3-92f9dedf9c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u + v}}\\left(3 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558781} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3257502-4734-5a4a-a6fe-1b87f74cb8aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a02015c9-3dc8-5d87-84de-d3d182e3bfdc" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x + y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le x - y\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le z\\le \\left(x - y\\right) \\left(x + y\\right)\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + y,\\quad v=x - y,\\quad w=\\sqrt{z}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le w\\le \\sqrt{u v}\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0\\\\1 & -1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(1)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}-(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & \\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ Since $z\\ge1$ and $w=\\sqrt z$, the branch is $w>0$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 \\sqrt{z}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 w\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 w\\right)\\left|- w\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{\\sqrt{u v}}\\left(3 w^{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162cdffd-9949-51f0-8443-68942a4348a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos^{2}{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558769} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec0dcc9-b1ef-5df4-b999-9593dcb919f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558783} {:answer/id #uuid "8c445ad6-15c1-5fd2-8beb-f3aae92262ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558792} {:answer/id #uuid "b77893e6-ffd7-5547-9f92-80985949652f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558797} {:answer/id #uuid "e1265fd1-1562-50c9-b768-fc4b94890233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u - v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558792} :answer-field/id #uuid "88af74af-81f7-52da-9877-69b3c31c5d10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca197872-f947-536a-b45e-7bafb14c0796" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 0\\le x + 3 y\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le - 3 y + z\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le z\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + 3 y,\\quad v=- 3 y + z,\\quad w=z,\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(x + z \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 y - z \\right)}\\,dV=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}\\left(\\boxed{?}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x + 3 y,\\quad v=- 3 y + z,\\quad w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\le w\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0\\\\0 & -3 & 1\\\\0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(1)\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=-3\\\\ &=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(x + z \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 y - z \\right)}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\right)\\left|- \\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{5}\\left(\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092324b9-324a-577d-8187-0a5286f7ef1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(\\frac{u}{2} + \\frac{3 w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558768} {:answer/id #uuid "384ceb69-cecb-55f9-aed6-e3bd5b768cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(\\frac{u}{2} + \\frac{3 w}{4}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558776} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c525f-3134-5b92-ab7a-9e88bd710485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(\\frac{v \\left(8 u + 3 w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558778} {:answer/id #uuid "60dff23d-d505-50f8-a62e-061ce9cf5786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(v^{2} \\left(8 u + 3 w\\right)\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558784} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a8a28d-7150-565f-9912-f59fafb22f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(\\frac{u}{2 v} + \\frac{3 w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558768} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cb431a1-e3de-516b-9ca4-9afe0bfddacf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cdc4fd63-3878-558d-af8d-65bb9f7d74a4" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): 1\\le x y\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le y\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z,\n$$\n\nWrite the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 2 x y^{2} + 3 y z\\,dV\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x y,\\quad v=y,\\quad w=4 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): 1\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 12\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}y & x & 0\\\\0 & 1 & 0\\\\0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(y)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(x)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0\\\\0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=4 y\\\\ &=4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4 v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ The sign of $v$ is fixed throughout $Δ$.\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 2 x y^{2} + 3 y z\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(8 u + 3 w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(\\frac{v \\left(8 u + 3 w\\right)}{4}\\right)\\left|\\frac{1}{4 v}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{12}\\left(\\frac{u}{2} + \\frac{3 w}{16}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b669794-92fe-524c-a98e-acbe50aa9636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558772} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6bed85-1056-5bf5-82f0-56e822e9105d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558785} {:answer/id #uuid "75e67673-79a6-51f7-aea1-a3287b24b545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558788} {:answer/id #uuid "bf26d959-5bea-5cbd-b529-22a8c2a6d4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558799} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8c848f-87b7-5107-8c5d-af33ba2dd461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558785} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6f2d4a-117f-5ba3-864f-ba275f597699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9a3dfe6-eaf9-53ba-8100-fedbe26e50f1" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z): \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\le 2 x + y\\le \\pi,\\quad 0\\le y + z\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad -1\\le z\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + z,\\quad w=z,\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x + y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y + z \\right)}\\,dV=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{-1}^{5}\\left(\\boxed{?}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:(u,v,w)\\longmapsto (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_Δ f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + z,\\quad w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1}$.\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y,z)\\longmapsto (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z)).\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v,w): \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\le u\\le \\pi,\\quad 0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad -1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z}\\\\\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0\\\\0 & 1 & 1\\\\0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=(2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(0)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\\\0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=2\\\\ &=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x + y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y + z \\right)}\\,dV &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\iiint_Δ \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\left|\\frac{1}{2}\\right|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw\\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{-1}^{5}\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ae34a70-04dd-51c4-956e-e0526d0f083b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558860} {:answer/id #uuid "b27db125-1811-506c-a9a7-73bd26f5a681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558896} {:answer/id #uuid "c881d35c-8be6-578a-ac79-fe3e965182f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558905} {:answer/id #uuid "f09c7440-efea-5620-96c8-cd2af8b7e0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558921} {:answer/id #uuid "f6645ca5-5ae8-52cc-a989-bcfcdd7ee0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558921} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2cc8718-1eda-50bf-b9d7-8164072450d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ec47881-0e4e-5e03-9b0e-45477c45f447" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e022d6-9b4b-55e1-b7b6-ebb206669d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558852} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8e6678-6b99-5782-a45c-738ef21e7fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558855} {:answer/id #uuid "24ba8bdc-8e30-56a6-ba09-574da792b447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558864} {:answer/id #uuid "8b39f368-2d1d-5ed3-9ac5-7ba0648583de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558888} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c28887-3141-5343-a694-92ac663af400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558864} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc4fd0a-f557-541f-8f97-4db32a398437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26c782a3-117c-52e4-894f-257331d9e2f3" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=9.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=1(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=1(36) \\\\ &=36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d774f4-7aa1-53ef-bfff-e5c57da8b9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558866} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf81fa8-fdda-58ba-a730-3d93182c45ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558876} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f5de92-b1be-5ef3-8c2e-9dd32a242b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558909} {:answer/id #uuid "df2bfe06-7bb3-5a53-b1bd-526ecc07d8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558914} {:answer/id #uuid "f867445f-eeb9-5bb8-ac4e-ccb29ea58e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558866} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd1ea61-ce15-535d-b216-c6fc2cd02f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "29d96b47-2528-56e8-a582-384c42fdef27" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=36.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=36.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=36\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 6,36).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=1(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=1(288) \\\\ &=288\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5760816a-e7c5-5515-ab4a-83eb16e46ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558877} {:answer/id #uuid "8aebe7d3-4a30-5378-ae62-21d1ea1bccc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558887} {:answer/id #uuid "8baa2b35-f9c3-5f1a-afb6-3b95bc5d3503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558889} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc885b9-d385-5924-9215-d1cc198f9347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558890} {:answer/id #uuid "99302851-9856-5f4e-b25b-65e31ac4175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558889} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd890be5-d378-5457-862c-49df09c9f099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cef64cc-06fc-5d5e-88c6-7d1cbbc17e0e" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029b9230-f734-5a97-aaf8-332a97157ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{144}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558851} {:answer/id #uuid "212837bb-d4a2-559b-b8ae-bdcf0fb8646e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{216}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558862} {:answer/id #uuid "9a398766-75ae-57bb-9edd-72b0e2c370b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{288}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558892} {:answer/id #uuid "c200dd04-cec0-5200-bf02-c61b8c34e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{72}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558899} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a64ba9-bcc3-5162-8fb6-51ca90c8a02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558923}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558923} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0641de9-51c8-5b0e-a004