 has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(288) \\\\ &=\\frac{144}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c77fe9a-3bb4-5a95-a9e4-ba4caec3f0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{250}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558861} {:answer/id #uuid "282f49f3-c8a0-5ec9-9e26-240a0adb3e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{250}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558867} {:answer/id #uuid "34aa0c62-866e-5716-bea9-13a164eb9cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558871} {:answer/id #uuid "479319fb-8a2c-5f38-a919-3a96a6d6d6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{500}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558875} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe1737a-a180-5975-ab3f-1947b931b375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1000}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558871} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4bd8f98-c703-54b2-ad2b-0833b3d8244b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6553e5e5-9cf8-5557-87c1-bd009f84e77d" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=25.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $5 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=25.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 5,25).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 25\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca54518b097929ecf8756bc699c5fef32de07b174134781e778fdcc395e964fe.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}5&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(7)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{7}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{25}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}[v]_{u^{2}}^{25}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}(25)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-5}^{5}(25 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 25 u]_{-5}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}(((125)+\\left(- \\frac{125}{3}\\right))-((-125)+\\left(\\frac{125}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{500}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{500}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127bf8cb-8d30-56a4-8bcf-c7895668bae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{512}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558857} {:answer/id #uuid "190a0830-4b78-5385-b252-e2f158e62cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558859} {:answer/id #uuid "668129e1-5de9-5e42-9b2a-0925f3b2a440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558882} {:answer/id #uuid "c656600f-54e3-5c1f-806f-8c472422e959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558903} {:answer/id #uuid "e03b6731-84e0-58b3-a508-69c0086297b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558903} :answer-field/id #uuid "b620d98e-346d-5dae-b84d-073286c98a47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b04467d5-9ec0-5dd3-9458-51db8f16665a" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
